octo-feather-lane: lane bookkeeping in this Herdr session's state dir (spec comms #lane-record).

  octo-feather-lane archive NAME   close lane NAME: move lanes/NAME.toml and
                                   lanes/NAME.handoffs.jsonl into lanes/archive/

The state dir is the one `octo-feather-state` prints (XDG_STATE_HOME, HERDR_SESSION).
Before moving any file `archive` runs the lane reflection, then the director reflection (`reflect`,
below); when either cannot run, the archive refuses naming the failed reflection, every file left in place.
`archive` creates lanes/archive/ if missing, moves each file byte-for-byte unchanged
and prints `archived: <file> sha256=<hex>` per file. A lane with no hand-off log gets one
from the reflection (its reflection line). It refuses, non-zero with every file left in
place, when the record is missing or either archive target already exists.

  octo-feather-lane reflect NAME   lane reflection (spec comms #reflection-lane): a fresh headless
                                   reflector answers the lane questions; appends its lines to the log

`reflect` gathers what the reflector cannot compute without bash: the record and hand-off log paths, the
director log's lines about the lane (its issue, or a text naming the lane, #N or ghN), the factory report's
lines for the lane (`report`, below), and the transcript paths of the supervisor, each worker the log names
with a session, and the director. It starts agents/reflector.md through pi-role with the lane questions of
reflection/questions.json, parses its labelled reply (form and refusals: scripts/octo-feather-reflect) and
appends to lanes/NAME.handoffs.jsonl (created when absent) its finding and reflection lines (spec comms
#reflection-output; fields: #handoff-kind-finding, #handoff-kind-reflection), in one write; prints
`reflection: ...` and each finding.
A reflector that cannot run or replies outside the form appends nothing: exit 1 naming the failed reflection.

  octo-feather-lane reflect --director [--due]
                                   director reflection (spec comms #reflection-director): a fresh
                                   headless reflector answers the director questions; appends its
                                   lines to the director log

`reflect --director` gathers the director log's lines since its last reflection line, the factory report
(`report`, below), the director's transcript path, every lane hand-off log (lanes/ and lanes/archive/) with its
lane reflection lines, and the finding class counts across those logs by source and frequency (spec comms
#reflection-writer-route). It runs the same reflector, parser and refusals as `reflect NAME`, with the director
questions, and appends to director.handoffs.jsonl its finding and reflection lines (spec comms
#reflection-output; fields: #handoff-director-finding, #handoff-director-reflection). One director reflection
runs at a time (a lock on the state dir). --due runs it only when one is due at a start (spec comms
#reflection-director; `octo-feather-start` runs that in the background) and the director log has lines, else
prints `director reflection: not due ...`, exit 0; also exit 0, nothing run, while another one runs.

Reflection source option (last): `--director-source CURRENT_DIRECTOR_PANE EXACT_ABSOLUTE_PI_SESSION_FILE`
is shared by `reflect NAME`, `reflect --director [--due]`, and `archive NAME`. A supervisor closes a
lane with `octo-feather-lane archive NAME --director-source PANE FILE`; both reflections use that
source. Native Herdr agent_session (id/path) is authoritative when present. Otherwise the option is
an explicit-director-handoff declaration through existing comms, not independently proven current
session ownership: an undetectably old same-pane/cwd declaration cannot be rejected. Record, live
Pi pane, source header/cwd and source prefix are validated/rechecked; native conflict or invalid
explicit input refuses atomically. Missing required director source is an unknown sourcegap and
refuses reflection/archive, never completion. --due's existing not-due cases still run no reflection.

After its reflection, `reflect NAME`, `reflect --director` (when it ran) and `archive` (after both, before any file
moves) file the correction lanes due (spec comms #reflection-correction; the rule: issue-lifecycle #lanes-correction)
through the tracker skill's `issue-create`, each in the tracker spec comms #reflection-correction names (a target's
through the current directory's tracker skill, GH_REPO its lane record's `repository`, refused naming the record that
spec section says is unfit); each issue's title carries the marker of
scripts/correction_lanes.py `MARK`, by which `gh issue list` finds the ones already filed. Prints
`correction lane: filed <key> in <tracker> for <class> (...)` or `correction lane: commented <url> on <key> ...` and
the comment per write (which write: spec comms #reflection-correction), else `correction lanes: none filed or commented`;
a tracker other than github-issues gets `correction lane: due, not filed: ...` naming the title to file by hand.
A tracker that cannot be read or written refuses the archive, its reflections' lines undone, every file in place.

Archive of a merged lane (issue 1134; issue-lifecycle #gates-main-moved, comms #transport-close-space): a lane record
with a `pr` first asks the lane's post-merge record (`post-merge`, below) for that PR. Steps 1-3 not recorded ok, the
archive runs the routine itself (PR, the recorded head or else the PR's, the record's `repository` as --repo), its
lines printed, and re-executes itself on the kit install when step 3 moved the install this script runs from; still
not ok, it refuses, `archive waits: step <n> <name> stopped: <line>`, exit 1, nothing archived. Then the reflections
and the move as above; then it records its part in the lane's post-merge record (close: the reflections' finding
counts, the correction lane lines, the tickets done and what is left open and where, as issue-view (of the tracker key
of the record's `issue`, as merge's done step maps it, below), `gh pr list
--base <lane-key>` and the Spec Chat registries read them then, and every line it printed). Its last act starts
step 8 in one transient unit outside the lane's workspace, as herdr-close stops every process of the closed pane's
agent scope (herdr-comms herdr_scope.py close): `systemd-run --user --unit=octo-feather-teardown-<lane>`, never a
service or timer, running `post-merge PR HEAD --lane <lane>` in the director's checkout (`git worktree list`, first
entry) with the archive's environment, bounded by RuntimeMaxSec 900, its lines appended to
$(octo-feather-state)/post-merge/<lane>.teardown.log, its end (any exit, killed or timed out) recorded by its stop
hook (ExecStopPost: `post-merge --lane <lane> --unit-end [--repo O/R]`, reading systemd's SERVICE_RESULT, EXIT_CODE,
EXIT_STATUS). Where no systemd user manager answers (`systemctl --user is-system-running`; macOS), the same run in
its own session (start_new_session, `post-merge PR HEAD --lane <lane> --unit-run`), killed past the same limit, then
the same end. Prints `teardown: started unit ...`. A unit that cannot start is named, told as a stop, exit 1. A
lane record with no `pr` archives as above alone.

  octo-feather-lane verdict PR HEAD REPORT|- [--resolution] [--repo O/R]
                                   create, or update in place, the PR's one review verdict
                                   comment (marker, head, verdict, check set, rounds, findings); prints its URL
  octo-feather-lane resolution MERGE
                                   the code reviewer's brief after a main merge that resolved a conflict or
                                   changed anything beyond git's automatic merge (when: issue-lifecycle
                                   #lanes-one-head): its files and what `git show --remerge-diff` prints for it
  octo-feather-lane merge-ask PR [--status FILE...] [--base REF] [--repo O/R]
                                   check the PR body's `## Merge ask` section; print it on pass
  octo-feather-lane ci-start PR [--status FILE...] [--base REF] [--wait SECONDS] [--repo O/R]
                                   CI on the lane's final head (when: issue-lifecycle #lanes-one-head): refuse unless
                                   the local HEAD is the PR head with a current full gate pass, mark the PR
                                   ready (gh pr ready), wait (default 120s) until CI's full run on it has
                                   started (when that is: #acceptance-ci-alongside) and print its jobs
  octo-feather-lane judge PR QUESTION --state FILE|- [--repo O/R]
                                   the kit's judgment model answers it on FILE's text; create, or
                                   update in place, the PR's one comment for QUESTION, labelled a model judgment

`judge` (spec judgment #boundary-advisory-judge) runs beside a code review: it refuses until the PR has
its verdict comment. QUESTION is a judgment suite's name without `judgment-` (test-quality, exact-or-proxy,
ticket-slice, call-evidence); every question is asked of the kit's one judgment model, evals/judgment-model.json
`model` (spec judgment#rule-one-model).
The caller names the state (a file, or - for stdin), in the question's state_shape (evals/judgment-<id>/question.json);
nothing is derived from prose. It runs through octo-feather-qa's classify path (the bench's Pi extension command,
no agent, in the qa sandbox; what it calls: octo-feather-qa --help; refused inside a check run), asking only that model.
The comment carries `Judgment: model judgment ...`, Question, Model, Answer, Confidence (the chosen label's
probability), Calibration (the question's held-out accuracy and Brier and its threshold from suite.json `calibration`, proposed or
accepted, and its status: below accepted threshold, no accepted threshold or meets accepted threshold; spec judgment
#rule-calibration), PR head, Review head (the verdict comment's), then the question and the state; prints its URL.
merge-ask, on pass, prints each such comment as one line under `Model judgments` after the section, beside the
review verdict; no gate reads one (spec judgment #rule-advisory): merge-ask passes or fails the same without it.

`verdict` writes through gh (never under the qc judge). REPORT is the code review's
report, a file or - for stdin. Its fields (the verdict, the check field, the Owner field) and the
reader scope: .agents/skills/code-review/SKILL.md step 4, **Report fields**. The last verdict line
in scope is the verdict, and lines that disagree are refused. What the check field classifies:
docs/adr/0008, Who builds what. Refused: no
verdict, neither check form, both, a malformed Check line, a class other than exact, proxy or declaration, and
a `class: proxy` line with `Verdict: clear` (a proxy check blocks; the declaration class: docs/adr/0008 rung 2). The comment's header carries
the lines; merge-ask prints them under the section for the owner. It also carries the report's owner decision
lines and `Opt-out cleared: <commit sha>` lines (form: Report fields, above; refused: an owner decision line with
nothing after its colon, a cleared value that is no sha), with those of the comment it replaces kept: only
merge-ask's tier reads them (merge-ask, Reasons). The header carries the report's `Ruling covers:
<url>` lines too, each the URL a spec call links as its ruling (scripts/octo-feather-spec-status, call log; refused
in another form): the reviewer's word that the owner's ruling covers that call's change (merge-ask's tier, below);
each post carries the report's own, never an earlier round's. HEAD is the head the review judged, H, a commit
of the PR (7+ hex prefix); the comment names it (`Head:`) and the PR's head when posted (`PR head:`).
Review rounds (rule: issue-lifecycle #lanes-one-head, #acceptance-verdict-covers-lane): the report's one `Range:`
line (its form: Report fields, above). Refused: no Range, `to` not HEAD, `from` not an ancestor of `to`, and a
`from` that is neither a commit of the PR's base branch (a full round) nor exactly the head the last posted round
ended on (a delta round), naming that head and the two ways out. The comment keeps every posted round under
`### Rounds` (`- Round N: <from>..<to>, full|delta, verdict V`, its check lines under it), a report of the last
round's range replacing that round; its header's `Check:` lines are the lane's check set: the latest full round's,
then each later delta's, a later line for the same path and rule replacing the earlier. A comment posted before
rounds counts as one round, the whole lane ending at its Head (`legacy`), so the next round may be a delta from
it. --resolution comments take no Range.
With --resolution, HEAD is such a merge, the review covering what `resolution` prints for it alone: the PR's one
comment for that merge names it (`Merge:`) and its resolution's files (`Files:`); refused for a commit that is no
merge or a merge with nothing beyond git's automatic merge.

  octo-feather-lane fields REPORT|-   a report's field lines as JSON (the parser verdict uses)

`fields` reads the lines in `verdict`'s reader scope (Report fields, above) and prints one JSON object:
"Check" {lines: [{path, rule, owner, reads, class}], none, errors} from the check field (errors:
`verdict`'s refusals, an absent field included); "Owner" {lines: [{path, rule, restated: [path...]}], none,
errors} from the Owner field (`:<line>` stripped from every path); "Decision" {owner: [line as written],
cleared: [sha], errors} from the owner decision and `Opt-out cleared:` lines (errors: `verdict`'s refusals); "fields" {Name: [value...]} for every
other `Name: value` line in scope (Name: a capital, then letters or -, in words one space apart: `Prior art`). Exit 0; 1 on an unreadable report.
The factory evals' field_lines fact runs it (scripts/octo-feather-qa).

`merge-ask` reads through `gh pr view --json` (also under the qc judge's offline gh) and
the git repo of the current directory (the lane worktree, PR head and BASE fetched; BASE
defaults to the PR's base branch on origin): the PR's commits and their subjects (BASE..head:
`gh pr view --json commits` lists at most 100; `verdict` reads them the same way), the judged
head's qc.toml for a lane with criterion rows, and the history when the PR head is not the judged head. The PR body carries the
parent line (spec issue-lifecycle #gates-pr-line): the target's tracker skill's `pr-line` for
the key its `key-from-branch` reads from the PR's branch (Closes #82 on GitHub, Tracks ENG-123
on Linear), and no other line closes a GitHub issue. The section, up to the next `## ` heading:

  ## Merge ask
  Head: <sha>                     the PR's head H' (7+ hex prefix)
  Checks: <summary>               verified: every check on that head SUCCESS, none pending; a SKIPPED one only
                                  as the judged head's green run, carried by the Judged proof (#lanes-one-head);
                                  none reported (an empty rollup, never a skipped check)
                                  passes only when both hold, else fails naming the one that does not or
                                  could not be read: the no-CI fact for that head (post-merge step 2), and
                                  the PR base's required-check policy requires no check: `gh api
                                  repos/<repo>/branches/<base>` protection.required_status_checks empty, and
                                  `gh api repos/<repo>/rules/branches/<base>` no required_status_checks rule
                                  (its plan-has-no-rulesets 403 counts as none). Printed after the section
                                  as `Checks: no CI: ...`, naming each fact and source
  Judged: <proof>                 = the line the check prints for H to H' (below)
  | criterion | qc verdict | judged | evidence |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | <spec>#<id> | pass | judged <sha> | <report path, command> |   one row per claimed criterion
  Review verdict: <comment URL>   this PR's verdict comment: head H, verdict clear
  Built: <id> <id>                = status entries built with this PR, each named by one of its `qc: built` commits
  Failed: none                    or `Failed:` then one `- <id>: <triage>` line per fail row

Every row names one judged head H (7+ hex), a commit of the PR: the head qc and the code
review both judged, in parallel (spec #lanes-one-head). H to H' may hold only (a) `qc: built
... (judged <sha>)` commits changing only the --status files and (b) merges of BASE that keep
the verdict by the rule of spec #lanes-one-head, the lane's own diff against BASE read from
BASE's merge base to the head, status files aside, hunk by hunk (what a file's hunks are:
scripts/merge_ask.py, _lane_diff), so main's changes elsewhere in a lane file keep it. Anything else fails naming the commit and
file: run just qc and the review again. `Judged:` is the proof: `<H12> is the PR head`, or
`<H12> to <H'12>:` the qc commits and files, and per main merge `lane hunks identical (N files, sha256 <12 hex>)`.
Ids are <spec slug>#<id> or a bare id one --status file names; pass --status once per spec.
Every lane's PR head needs a current recorded full gate pass (the rule: spec #lanes-checkpoints): the record
`octo-feather-gate passed` prints for it, or for a lane commit it stands for by the same H to H' check, whose
record is clean and whose gate_config and list are the head's own (`octo-feather-gate config`); else `Full pass:` fails naming the head.
Insufficient evidence (quality spec #qc-results) is never triaged: the check fails naming each criterion whose row
says it, or whose latest result in the qc reports at the judged head says it (octo-feather-qc --help, Report;
OCTO_FEATHER_QC_DIR as there), with what is missing, until a rerun of just qc gives it pass or fail.
The verdict comment must also carry its check classes (`Checks: none` or `Check:` lines, no
`class: proxy`), and its rounds (verdict, above) must chain from BASE to its Head: walking back from the last round
while each starts where the one before it ended, the first is a full round starting off the PR's commits or a
legacy comment; else the check names the range the rounds cover and the lane before them no posted round covers.
Pass prints them under `Review rounds`, after `Review check classes`. Pass: the section on stdout, then `Decision sources`, one line per row: the decision source
of the criterion's latest result in the qc reports at the judged head (as for insufficient evidence, above), or
`no qc result at the judged head`, shown only (quality spec #qc-results); `Review check classes` (below); then `Gate time`, the full gate on the final head (the passed
full run, check-runs.jsonl in the state dir, on the PR head or the latest lane commit before it that has one)
against gate-budget.toml at the PR head, in the report's lines (report, above; the trend: passed full runs on the
head's history), flagged and shown only: the code reviewer judges it, no gate reads it, and the ask passes or
fails the same without it (spec issue-lifecycle #lanes-checkpoints; on a failure the block goes to stderr before
the items). `Review check classes` lists the comment's
lines for the owner, its `class: declaration` lines (the class: docs/adr/0008 rung 2) under `Declaration checks (stopgaps; owner rules
at merge): <n>` after it (how: spec issue-lifecycle #lanes-one-head), shown only (send it all with herdr-say --parent). Failure: each missing item
with its fix on stderr, exit 1. A pass ends with `next: octo-feather-lane merge <PR> <head>` on stderr, outside
what is sent: merge (below) decides whether it goes.

The tier (spec issue-lifecycle #gates-tiers, #gates-tier-relay): every ask is sorted into tier 1, 2 or 3, the
highest any reason gives (no reason: 1), printed on a pass right after the section's lines, on stdout:

  Tier: N
  - <reason>                      one line per reason
  Waits for: nobody | the director's order (PR #N: <why>) | the owner

Reasons (tier): each criterion the section's `Failed:` lists, with its triage (3); each line of the section that
starts at column 0 with `Owner decision` and holds a colon (`Owner decision: <text>`, `Owner decision 1 (<what>):
<text>`), shown as written (3): any agent raises the tier by adding one; each such line in the header of the
verdict comment `Review verdict:` links (verdict, above), shown as written (3); each behaviour-neutral opt-out
the kit's check-behavior-smoke prints for the lane (`--base` the merge base of the head and BASE, `--head` the
head: its `opt-out <sha>` lines), `opt-out <sha> cleared by the review verdict: Behaviour-neutral: <reason>`
when that comment is clear and an `Opt-out cleared:` line in its header names the commit (2), else `opt-out <sha>
not cleared by the review verdict: ...` (3); opt-outs that could not be read, naming why (3); each `Check:` line
of that comment's header classed `class: declaration` (Declaration checks, above) when the comment is clear,
`declaration check: <the line after `Check: `>` (3): the owner rules on it before the merge; each spec call the lane diff adds (a
line of a `*.calls.jsonl` the diff from the merge base to the PR head changes, read at both ends with git show,
not at the merge base: the same call with or without `denied`, as `octo-feather-spec-status calls` counts it),
named `spec call <slug> <at> <kind> <anchors>`: denied by the owner, `: denied <at>` (2); clarifies (2); any
other kind (3, `: no ruling linked`), unless its `ruling` URL's issue or issue comment was created before its
`at` (GitHub's created_at, `gh api ... --jq .created_at`) and the header of the review verdict comment the
section links carries `Ruling covers: <that URL>` (verdict, above): then `: ruled by <url> (<created_at>), the
review verdict covers it` (2); else the reason it is not, `ruling <url> is no GitHub issue or issue comment
URL`, `not read (...)`, `made <created_at>, not before the call` or `not confirmed: the review verdict carries no
...` (3); a log not read, `spec calls not
read: <why>` (3); each path the lane changes (git diff from
the merge base of the head and BASE to the head, a rename as its two paths) whose owners in the code owners file at the
head or at the merge base (each end's first of .github/CODEOWNERS, CODEOWNERS, docs/CODEOWNERS, GitHub's order; either
only raises, so a lane editing it never lowers its own tier; none at either: no path) include `@<owner>`,
the owner login of --repo O/R (else of `gh repo view`; an `@org/team` never counts): `code owner: <path> is @<owner>'s
(<file>[ at the merge base <sha12>] line N: `<line>`)` (3), the head's file named first. The line is the last one matching the path, as GitHub takes it, each line matched
by git's own matcher (`git check-ignore --no-index`, one line at a time: scripts/merge_ask.py, _owned). A line
of either file whose pattern git reads otherwise than GitHub (each such syntax and why: merge_ask.CODE_OWNERS_DIFFER) fails the
ask, `code owners: <file> line N `<line>`: <why>; ...`, each named, and no path is sorted. A tier 3 ask waits for the owner;
a tier 1 or 2 ask whose merge order (below) names an open PR this merge sets back (its head conflicts with this head,
or this merge stales its pinned run), or one of whose reads failed (a `- not read:` line: it cannot prove none is set
back), waits for the director's order, naming each (`PR #N: <why>`, `PR #N: its pinned run <run> not read`,
`not read: <what>`; `; ` between), its tier unchanged; else for nobody. A failing ask prints the block on stderr alone, after the line `Incomplete
merge ask (it fails below; never send this tier):`, after the merge order and before the items, never on stdout.

`Merge order` (spec issue-lifecycle #gates-merge-order), shown only, after `Gate time` on a pass, and on stderr
after it, before the items, on a failure (before the refusal when nothing can be judged): the ask passes or fails
the same without it. It names each other open PR on the PR's base (`gh pr list --state open --base <base>`), its
head fetched (`git fetch origin pull/N/head`) when the repo lacks it, that this merge would set back: `- PR #N
(<branch>): its head conflicts with this head in <files>` (git merge-tree of the two heads), and `- PR #N
(<branch>): this merge stales its pinned run RUN/SUITE/CASE: <items>` for each `octo-feather-qa --verdict` pin its
qc.toml demos name at its head (none without a qc.toml), the items that command names stale for it at BASE merged
with this head (a git commit-tree, no ref moved) and not at BASE, each run in a scratch detached worktree
(freshness: octo-feather-qa --help, Verdict). A read that fails, or a pin that command judges no freshness for, is
one `- not read: ...` line; nothing named prints `- none: <n> other open pull requests on <base>, none this merge
would stale or conflict with`.

A correction lane (spec issue-lifecycle #lanes-correction, #1226): the parent issue (key-from-branch, read with
the tracker skill's issue-view) carries `reflect`'s title marker `[correction-lane <class>]`, and the verdict
comment lists a Check line. Its PR body then carries a `## Replay` section, a table `| finding | case | command |
result |` read as GitHub renders it (markdown-it-py, merge_ask.REPLAY_TABLE: a table in a fence or an HTML
comment is none, `\|` is a pipe in its cell, each of the four cells filled), one row per finding (id
`<lane> <at>`, from the finding lines of the issue's body and comments as
`reflect` quotes them: `- <lane> <at> <source>: ...`), or one row `trigger` when it quotes none, and one row
`clean <lane>` per lane of the clean sample: lanes archived in the state dir (lanes/archive) whose record's
`repository` is the PR's, whose hand-off log's last line is dated before the PR's first commit (its earliest
committer time) and holds no finding of the class, newest last line first, 5 (fewer if fewer exist). Result:
`caught`, `missed`, `pass` or `not replayable: <why>`. merge-ask runs each other row's command itself, only
ever inside the qc sandbox, each row in its own (octo-feather-qc sandboxed(), the shape its demos run in: `sh -c`
at this worktree's top, which must be at the PR head; writes discarded, no network, no Pi, 120 s at most) with
the offline read-only gh over one GitHub snapshot per merge ask (octo-feather-qc github_snapshot(), naming the
parent issue and each `#N` of the rows' case cells, so older tickets are in full): its program must be a path on
a verdict Check line; `caught` holds on a non-zero exit whose output names the class, `missed` and `pass` on exit
0. A row the sandbox cannot serve (the worktree not at the PR head, no snapshot, the sandbox unavailable or its
proof failed, an attempt it refused: an offline gh refusal, a write to GitHub included, a masked path (Pi's
sessions included) or a host, or 120 s passed) is shown `not replayable: <reason>`, never judged and never run
outside it. Stated limit: the sandbox unsets the caller's Herdr pane (HERDR_PANE_ID) and masks Pi's sessions, so a
check that needs the pane (`skills`) cannot run there; its rows declare `not replayable: <reason>`. It fails naming each
finding and clean lane with no row (all of them with no section), each row whose program is on no Check line,
and each whose claim differs from its run, with the run's exit and the end of its output. Pass prints
`Replay: caught k of n (m not replayable); false refusals f of c clean` (c: clean rows run, f: those caught)
after `Review rounds`, then one line per row: what its run showed and the worktree writes the sandbox
discarded; a low k is never refused: the owner judges it. Not detected: a parent with no marker.

A lane that builds no criterion (spec #lanes-one-head) says `Built: none` and `Failed: none` with no
criterion row (no table, or its header alone), and its PR holds no `qc: built` commit and no --status
entry built with it; --status is then optional. Its judged head H is the review verdict comment's Head:
the check needs Head, Checks, the parent line and Review verdict, and `Judged:` only when H is not the
PR head (the same proof as above).

A review verdict on a head before the judged head (or, building no criterion, before the PR head) holds when the
same proof carries it there, with each main merge that resolved a conflict or changed anything beyond git's
automatic merge allowed when a clear `verdict --resolution` comment names it, and only its resolution's files moved
in the lane diff; the section then needs `Reviewed: <proof>`, the line the check prints, naming the reviewed head,
each merge, its files and its resolution verdict's URL. Without it the check names the merge. A lane that claims any criterion (a Built or Failed id, a qc commit,
a status entry built with the PR) with no row is refused naming the criterion table.

  octo-feather-lane tickets PARENT check every sub-issue of ticket PARENT as a tracer bullet
  octo-feather-lane ticket FILE|-  check one ticket draft (a file, or stdin) before publishing

`tickets` and `ticket` (spec issue-lifecycle #acceptance-tracer-tickets) read a ticket's
`## Acceptance criteria` list. A ticket passes when it names at least one criterion id
`<spec slug>#<anchor>` whose anchor is the data-anchor of a data-acceptance-criterion
element in docs/specs/<slug>.spec.html (of the git repo at the current directory; a story,
section or table anchor is no criterion; <template> content: #121 D17), or, serving none, names the rule it
serves and that rule's owner (spec #lanes-tickets) in an item `rule: <rule> (<owner>)`, the `<rule> (<owner>)`
form of the review's Check lines (docs/adr/0008): the rule non-empty, the owner an ADR of the repo
(`docs/adr/NNNN...` or `ADR NNNN`, naming exactly one docs/adr/NNNN-*.md) or `owner ruling <date and/or issue>`;
a rule item with no owner, an unknown ADR, an empty rule or another owner fails class unowned-rule, and
beside a criterion id it is allowed, not required. Its last item is `demo: ` with a backticked command that runs
the repo's own public surface (ADR 0009): after env assignments and runners (uv run, npx,
pnpm exec...), a program the repo declares (a tracked executable outside tests/, a justfile
recipe other than check/check-*/integrate/qc/qa as `just --dump --dump-format json` lists it:
imports, aliases and `just MOD RECIPE` included, never a header regex; no just or a justfile
it cannot read is exit 2, #172; the recipes are the ones just itself says it runs, in the repo,
dependencies included, none a gate or factory recipe: just parses the words beside --completions, and
unless it objects to more than their recipe words and overrides (a subcommand such as --show, --list or
--command runs no recipe), dry-runs them with --verbose, naming each recipe; executing nothing,
evaluating no backtick; every option, parameter and default is just's to read, and anything just refuses,
-q with --dry-run included, or a dry run naming no recipe (the just pinned in the kit's mise.toml is
the one whose answer this reads), fails naming just's answer; check-gate-free's just_runs, #240), a package.json bin or pyproject
[project.scripts] name; a tracked script run by its interpreter, e.g. `python harness.py`,
when program() finds none of these: the ADR 0002 fallback, #121 D11), curl/http to an http(s) URL, or playwright. A demo
through MCP or another public protocol runs a client the repo declares by the same rule, e.g. a justfile recipe
`mcp-call` that runs the protocol's client against the repo's server; never the gate alone
or a generic tool (printf, echo). The
wide-refactor line `Expand-contract: expand|migrate|contract` waives the demo rules. A demo may run a
command the ticket itself creates when the ticket declares it exactly in a line
``Creates: `<path>` `` (repo-relative, the script or file the demo runs, e.g. scripts/new-cmd or bin/x;
a leading ./ is dropped), one backticked value, the line repeatable: a demo stage passes when its
program, after env assignments and runners, is exactly that path (written with a /, e.g. ./run for a
top-level file, and stands for itself, never an interpreter or HTTP tool by its basename). A declared
path that already exists still passes. A just recipe is never declared (owner ruling #240, C): an
existing one is a demo as above, needing no Creates; one the justfile lacks is created in an earlier
ticket, or the demo runs the path it runs. Class bad-creates: a value not one backticked path, empty,
`just ...`, absolute, with `..`, not normalized, or under tests/ or a hook dir. Class unused-creates: a declared path no demo stage runs (an
Expand-contract ticket runs none). A demo of an undeclared command the repo lacks stays
layer-only-ticket, its fix naming the Creates form. A ticket body
is read outside column-0 ``` or ~~~ fences and HTML comments `<!--` to `-->`; no lists, indented code
or code spans; a literal `<!--` in prose opens a comment (#145, #121 D18 D21). Checks are
structural facts only, never what the prose means: whether the demo shows the behavior What to
build describes is review's and the qc judge's (docs/adr/0008, #118). Each failure prints
`<ticket>: class: <class>: <problem>; fix: <fix>`. Exit 0 all pass, 1 a ticket fails,
2 nothing could be checked (gh, file, specs or justfile error; nothing printed as passed).
`tickets` reads tickets only through the target's tracker skill's `issue-view` (the parent's
`children`, then each body; GitHub Issues or Linear, from the .octo-feather.toml of the git repo
at the current directory; on GitHub it works under the qc judge's offline gh).

A ticket's skills (spec issue-lifecycle #lanes-tickets): one line at column 0, outside fences and HTML comments as
the body is read above, `Skills: <skill>, <skill>` (a name may be backticked; a trailing .;:!? is no part of it)
or `Skills: none`, under the to-tickets template's `## Skills` heading; with several, the last counts. Every ticket
carries one (owner ruling 2026-11-19 on
#1099): none is class missing-skills. `ticket` reads the draft's line; `tickets`, as `skills`, the latest line of
each ticket's body and comments (a line set later is a comment; `issue-view KEY --notes`, as `seam` reads them, by
each note's last change), so a later comment's line replaces the body's and is the one checked. Each name must be a skill agents/implementer.md is offered as pi-role
resolves its skills line, {tracker} as the target's tracker skill; else class unknown-skill, one line per name.
Transition, as check-owner-field's: `tickets` passes a ticket with no line anywhere whose body was written (its
last change, from that notes read) before the commit adding the rule (the kit's commit adding the SKILLS_SINCE line,
git log -S, its committer time), printing `<ticket>: ok; notice: no `Skills:` line, body written <time>, before the
rule (<sha12>, <time>): not checked; the line it owes: ...`; a kit with no such history passes it the same, naming
that. A draft (`ticket`) has no such time: it always fails with no line.

  octo-feather-lane seam TICKET <spec>#<anchor>|rule SEAM [--before REV]
                                   is SEAM written down for that criterion, or its rule, on TICKET (before REV)

`seam` checks the `Seam:` record contract (skills spec #skill-tdd, #skill-to-tickets; AGENTS.md Rules,
integration test seam; correction lanes #987, #1121). It reads TICKET through the tracker skill's `issue-view KEY --notes`
(skills spec #tracker-script-issue-view). A record is a line at column 0, outside fences and HTML comments as
`ticket` reads them, `Seam: <spec>#<anchor>: `<seam>`` (the colon after the id optional), then any note; the seam
is its first code span, compared exactly (`herdr-spawn` is not `scripts/herdr-spawn`). Anything else naming a
seam (a table, prose) is no record: whether it agreed a seam stays with review. <spec>#<anchor> must be an item of
the body's `## Acceptance criteria`, else class seam-off-criteria, exit 1 (with --before, a note: below). A ticket serving a rule (an item `rule:
<rule> (<owner>)`, as `ticket` reads it) records `Seam: rule: `<seam>``, checked with the id `rule`: one record for
the ticket's rules, else exactly as a criterion's; `rule` on a ticket with no `rule:` item is seam-off-criteria. Without --before: exit 0 when a note
holds the record, printing `<ticket>: seam <id> `<seam>` recorded at <time> (<url>)`. With --before REV (the
commit adding the test, in the git repo here; its author time, git %aI, kept through rebase and cherry-pick), per
note holding the record: before when it was written and last changed (null: never) strictly before REV; formed
after REV when it was written at or after REV; unknown when it was written before REV and its text changed at or
after: the record may or may not have been there at REV. Exit 0 when one note is before (printed as above, `,
before commit <sha>`); else 2, printing `<ticket>: unchecked: ...`, when one is unknown; else exit 0 with one line
`<ticket>: note, class unlisted-test-seam: <what>`, <what> the record's time and url and the commit's, or no record
on the ticket, or the criterion the ticket does not list: --before reports and never refuses on timing (owner
ruling on #1230); review judges the seam by its kind (quality#acceptance-public-surfaces). Without --before, no
record exits 1, class unlisted-test-seam, printed as `ticket` prints its failures. A writer runs it before the
first test at a seam, and with --before on the commit adding one. Exit 2, unchecked,
also when nothing could be read: usage, git, or issue-view's error (a tracker that cannot give a time exits
non-zero naming it); never reported as late. Whether SEAM is the seam the test drives stays with review
(docs/adr/0008 rung 3).

  octo-feather-lane skills TICKET  TICKET's Skills: line against its worker's Pi session, at hand-off

`skills` (spec issue-lifecycle #lanes-tickets, #acceptance-ticket-skills) is run by the supervisor when it consumes
a worker's result, in its own pane (HERDR_PANE_ID) and the lane worktree. It reads TICKET as `seam` does
(`issue-view KEY --notes`) and takes the latest `Skills:` line of the body and its comments (by each note's last
change; form: ticket, above). The worker's session: the latest spawn or worker-spawn line whose ticket is TICKET in
the caller's hand-off log (found as octo-feather-handoff spawn --caller finds it: the lane record whose `supervisor`
is the pane, else the director log), matched by lane key: the target's tracker skill's `lane-key` gives the line's
ticket and TICKET (issue-view's key) one lane key, never a prefix stripped (herdr-spawn's ticket is the worker
name's, so `implementer-gh40` is 40's, `implementer-eng-123` ENG-123's; a ticket lane-key refuses is no ticket's);
its `session` the Pi session file sessions/*/*_<session>.jsonl (PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR, else
~/.pi/agent), read by the factory report's skill-reach reader (report, skill reach: a load is a read or bash call
that opened <skill>/SKILL.md and did not fail, never a mention), the whole session. Prints
`<ticket>: worker <name> (<pane>), Pi session <file>`, then per named skill `<ticket>: skill <skill>: loaded`,
`cannot tell (<call> names its SKILL.md; ...)`, `not loaded` (offered, never read) or `not offered` (not in the
session's offered list, never read). Cannot tell: never read, but a call that did not fail, as a read is judged
(top level, or a codemode batch's nested call; a batch with a nestedCalls record is judged by those calls alone),
names its SKILL.md in its arguments: `<folder>/SKILL.md` (never SKILL.md.patch) with the folder literal, or a computed folder piece ($var,
glob, brace) with the folder a word of the call (a bare `*` names every folder); a write or edit names none.
Each cannot-tell appends one finding line {at, kind: finding, source: skills, class: skills-unchecked, blocking:
false, summary (naming the call), ticket, skill} in the same write as the misses and prints `<ticket>: <N>
non-blocking finding line(s) in <log>, class skills-unchecked: ...`; it never sends back. Each skill neither
loaded nor cannot-tell is a miss: one finding line per miss appended to that log in one write (comms spec #handoff-kind-finding):
{at, kind: finding, source: skills, class: skill-not-loaded, blocking, summary, ticket, skill}. A miss sends the
worker back once (owner ruling 2026-11-19 on #1099), counted by that log alone: the first miss of a ticket and skill
(no skills finding with blocking true for them in the log) logs blocking true, prints `<ticket>: fix: send <worker>
back before integrate: herdr-say <pane> '...'` and exits 1; a later miss of the same ticket and skill logs blocking
false, prints `<ticket>: skill <skill>: sent back once already; this miss stands recorded, not blocking` and, when
no other skill is a first miss, exits 0, or 2 with a cannot-tell. Exit 0 also with no miss and no cannot-tell, with `Skills: none` or with no line (nothing
compared; `ticket` and `tickets` fail the last). Exit 2, could not check, never blocking (supervisor ruling on
#1099: only a real miss, exit 1, sends the worker back): a tracker error, no HERDR_PANE_ID, a pane owning no record,
no spawn line with a session for TICKET, not exactly one session file, or one it cannot read. It names the cause and
appends one line to the caller's hand-off log when it finds one, {at, kind: finding, source: skills, class:
skills-unchecked, blocking: false, summary, ticket}, saying `recorded in <log>` or `not recorded (<why>)`; a usage
error records nothing.
The limit: it proves a load, never that the skill was applied, nor that the line named the right skills; review
judges those.

  octo-feather-lane done PARENT    the lane's close (spec issue-lifecycle #gates-pr-line): move
                                   every ticket of PARENT, then PARENT, to done

`done` runs the tracker skill's `issue-done` on each sub-issue `issue-view PARENT` lists, then
on PARENT, whether or not the tracker already did (one already done stays as it is), reads each
back with `issue-view` and prints `<key>: done`. A ticket that fails or does not read done is
named with its reason on stderr, after the others were tried; exit 1, rerun after the fix.

  octo-feather-lane spend          the spend ledger's totals, then the OpenRouter reconcile
  octo-feather-lane spend record [--lane NAME]
                                   append the model calls given as JSON lines on stdin to the ledger

`spend` (spec spend) reads the ledger, $(octo-feather-state)/spend.jsonl: append-only JSON lines, one per
model call, {at, lane, purpose, model, input_tokens, output_tokens, cost_usd}. It prints totals by UTC day,
lane, purpose, lane and purpose (each role under its lane) and model: dollars, calls, tokens, and calls with
unknown cost (cost_usd null) counted apart (spec spend#rule-cost). Then the reconcile, per window: OpenRouter's usage for the key, the ledger's dollars
on openrouter/* models over the same window, and the gap = OpenRouter minus ledger (spec spend#rule-gap). The
windows are OpenRouter's key usage windows (GET /api/v1/key usage_daily, usage_weekly, usage_monthly,
openrouter.ai/openapi.json): today = the current UTC day from 00:00Z, this week = the current UTC week starting
Monday 00:00Z, this month = the current UTC calendar month from day 1 00:00Z. The key is OPENROUTER_API_KEY,
else `openrouter.key` in ~/.pi/agent/auth.json (a `!command` key runs the command, as Pi does); it is never
printed: `curl` makes the call, the key on its stdin config, never in argv. No key, or an
unreachable or failing endpoint, prints the reason in the reconcile section; the totals still print, exit 0.
No budget: nothing is blocked (spec spend#rule-no-budget).

`spend record` is the ledger's one writer: each call path appends through it. Each stdin line is a JSON object:
model (provider/id, e.g. openrouter/qwen/qwen3-8b) and purpose (e.g. bench, judge, a role name), strings;
input_tokens and output_tokens, integers >= 0; cost_usd, the provider's reported cost in USD, or null or absent
when unreported; at, optional ISO 8601 time of the call (default now, UTC). The lane is --lane NAME (for callers
that lost the HERDR_* env, e.g. in a sandbox), else the lane record in $(octo-feather-state)/lanes/*.toml whose
`supervisor` pane is in the caller's Herdr space (HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID, e.g. w37 for supervisor "w37:p2", else
OCTO_FEATHER_SPEND_SPACE: scripts/pi-role --help, Headless), else
null: no lane (spec spend#rule-lane). Records only grow (spec spend#rule-append): all lines of one call are
appended in one locked write, so concurrent writers never interleave. It never fails its caller: a line it
cannot read, or a ledger it cannot write, prints one stderr line naming the unrecorded spend, and it exits 0
(spec spend#rule-write-fails).

  octo-feather-lane intake         every open correction-lane issue by class, with the correction-lane rule's
                                   evidence (spec comms #reflection-correction); reads only, writes nothing

`intake` lists the correction-lane issues (title marker, as `reflect` files them) of the github-issues trackers of
every lane record's `repository` (lanes/ and lanes/archive/) and the kit's, through the same `gh issue list` call
as `reflect`; an issue filed in another tracker (Linear) is not found (gh only). Per class of an open one:
`class <class>: due yes|no; blocking <n> in <m> lane(s) (<lanes>); findings <k>`, under it each open issue
(`issue <repo>#<n>: <title>`), `duplicates: <n> open issues of class <class>` when two or more, and each finding
line of the class in the lane logs and the director log (`finding <lane|director> <at> <source> blocking|not
blocking: <summary>`). Due and what counts: scripts/correction_lanes.py `corrections`, `counts`. Exit 0; 1 when
gh cannot list a tracker.

  octo-feather-lane report         every open lane of this session, then the owner queue and owner-caught

What `report` reads and shows, that it writes nothing, and how it treats a source it cannot read:
docs/specs/comms.spec.html#factory-report. Its output, `report at <now> (<lanes dir>)`, then the headline (spec comms
#report-owner-caught):

  owner-caught (headline, director.handoffs.jsonl): <N> in the last 7 days, <M> in the 7 days before
                                                 by each finding's at; `; <K> with no readable at, not counted`
                                                 when some have none; `log none` with no log, `unread` when unreadable

then per lane:

  lane <record name>: issue <issue> <repository> "<goal>"
    started: <started_at>                        its age is the lane's wall time
    pr: #N <state> <draft|ready> head <12 hex> checks: <count> <result>, ...   (`pr: none` with no pr)
    running: <pane> <name> <Herdr state>, one line per Herdr agent whose workspace is the supervisor
             pane's prefix; `(supervisor)` marks the supervisor's pane, a supervisor pane not listed is said
    waits: waiting_on "<value>", last hand-off <last_handoff_at>
    waits: worker <name> <pane> working          per agent other than the supervisor in state working
    waits: owner gate <gate> asked <at>
    waits: check <name> <status> on head <12 hex>
    review rounds: <N>                           the hand-off log's `"kind": "review"` lines
    spend: $<dollars> over <N> calls of known cost, <M> calls of unknown cost
                                                 spend.jsonl lines whose lane is the record name
                                                 (cost_usd null: unknown; neither a number nor null:
                                                 unparsable); `none recorded for <lane>`,
                                                 `no spend recorded (spend.jsonl: none)` with no ledger
    gate time: lane <total_s>s exit <exit>, <N> steps, head <12 hex>, started <start>
    gate time: main <total_s>s exit <exit>, <N> steps, head <12 hex>, started <start>
                                                 the check-runs.jsonl line with the latest start whose
                                                 tier is full (a subset run, just fast's, never stands in;
                                                 a line with no tier, written before the runners recorded
                                                 one, counts as today) and whose
                                                 head is a commit of the PR (`gh pr view --json commits`),
                                                 then of the default branch's last 100 commits (one
                                                 `gh api repos/<repository>/commits` per repository);
                                                 `<lane|main> none: <why>` with no such run

After the lanes:

  gate budget <owner/name>: the latest passed full gate run against gate-budget.toml on <owner/name>'s default branch
    full gate: <s>s, head <12 hex>, started <start>: OVER BUDGET by <s>s (budget <s>s)
                                                 or `within budget, <s>s to spare (budget <s>s)`, `no budget (<why>)`
    growth: <+x.y%> over the trend's median <s>s (the <N> passed full runs before it); set share <p>%: GROWN
                                                 or `within`; `no set share (<why>)` with no budget
    trend: <s>s <12 hex> <start>, ... (passed full runs, oldest first)
    slowest: <name> <s>s, ...                    the latest run's 5 slowest items
                                                 one block per open lane's repository (spec comms
                                                 #report-field-gate-time): check-runs.jsonl lines with tier full
                                                 and exit 0 (each runner's trend writer sets tier: the kit's
                                                 tests/gate/conftest.py and its copy templates/python/tests/
                                                 conftest.py for pytest, templates/typescript/tests/trend-
                                                 reporter.ts for Vitest; a line without it is no full run
                                                 here) whose head is a commit of
                                                 the default branch (the same 100) or of its open lanes' PRs;
                                                 the latest is the full gate time, trend_runs before it the
                                                 trend; `gate budget <repo>: no passed full gate run ...` or
                                                 `unread: ...` instead. Shown only: no gate reads it
  owner queue:                                   (`owner queue: none` with none pending)
    lane <record name> gate <gate> asked <at>    oldest first
  owner-caught (director.handoffs.jsonl):        (`: none` with no such finding; `owner-caught: log ...: none` with no log)
    class <class>: count <N>, last seen <at>     by class name
  director reflection: last ran <at>             the director log's latest reflection line (`never ran (<log>)`
                                                 with none; `never ran (<log>: none)` with no log)
  skill reach (Pi sessions active in the last 7 days; per role: skill read/offered, sessions):
    <role> (<N> sessions): <skill> <read>/<offered>, ...; read, not offered: <skill> <sessions>, ...
                                                 spec comms #report-skill-reach, one line per role, by name:
                                                 the Pi session files (PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR, else ~/.pi/agent,
                                                 then sessions/*/*.jsonl) with a line timestamped in the last 7
                                                 days, whenever they started (a file last written before that is
                                                 not opened). Role: the first `# ` heading of the system prompt's
                                                 appended role prompt (`no role` without one). Offered: its
                                                 <available_skills> names, whenever that prompt was written.
                                                 Read: a read or bash call (top level, or one a codemode batch
                                                 dispatched) whose result is timestamped in the last 7 days and
                                                 did not fail (top level: its result's isError not true; in a
                                                 batch: the call's status ok in the result's nestedCalls, as Pi
                                                 records them) and whose own arguments open <folder>/SKILL.md:
                                                 the read tool's path; in bash (shell words; # opens a comment
                                                 only at a word's start, as in bash), a file operand of
                                                 cat, head, tail, sed, less, more, bat or nl, or a < input (not
                                                 sed's script text, an option's value, a > target, a heredoc
                                                 body, sed -i). A codemode batch's source is never read: a batch
                                                 with no nestedCalls record counts no read. A path in text, a
                                                 pattern, a grep, a write or edit is no read (skills: cannot tell), and a computed
                                                 path ($var, ${...}, a glob) is not resolved, so not counted:
                                                 the count may miss reads, never makes one up. A folder an
                                                 offered skill's location names counts as
                                                 that skill (a linked skill by its offered name), any other by
                                                 the folder. A session counts once per skill; a read of a skill
                                                 the session was not offered counts apart. `... none` with no
                                                 session; `skill reach: no Pi session (sessions <dir>: none)`
                                                 with no folder; an unreadable file or one with no session line
                                                 first: `  unread: Pi session <dir>/<file>: <reason>`
  spend.jsonl: <N> unparsable line(s) (<line numbers>)       when it has any; likewise check-runs.jsonl

gate-budget.toml, a repository's gate-time budget at its root (read from its default branch, `gh api
repos/<repository>/contents/gate-budget.toml`; by merge-ask at the PR head), kept by the factory like any repo
policy: TOML, read with the standard library, a file of its own so a budget change changes no gate configuration
(octo-feather-gate config) and no qc contract. Keys: budget_seconds (a number > 0), growth_share (a number >= 0:
GROWN when the run is more than that share over the trend's median), trend_runs (an integer >= 1: that many passed
full runs before the latest make the trend; 5 shown with no file); optional test_cpu_seconds (a number > 0: the
per-test CPU budget the gate's runner reads, tests/gate/conftest.py; not read here); optional heavy_items,
heavy_slots, eval_slots, slot_grace_seconds, slot_bound_seconds (the box's run slots: octo-feather-slot --help;
not read here); optional fast_workers and fast_runs (the fast tier's worker count and how many fast-tier
runs run at once on the box: tests/gate/heavy_slot.py; not read here).
Anything else is `unread: gate-budget.toml (<repo>)`.

Every time prints as its UTC instant and `(<age> ago)` at the report's instant. A source it cannot
read prints as `unread: <source>: <reason>` where its facts would be; a log's unparsable lines are
counted and named by line number. The last line is `unread: none` or `unread: <source>, ...`.
Exit 0; 1 on any argument.

  octo-feather-lane merge PR HEAD [--wait SECONDS] [--repo O/R]
                                   the supervisor's merge of its lane's PR at the HEAD its merge ask named, then
                                   `done` on the lane's parent and the post-merge routine for the lane

`merge` (issue 1133, 1266; issue-lifecycle #gates-main-moved, #gates-merge-guard) merges only what its merge ask's
tier or the director's relay allows (allowed, below). In order, each refusal before the merge exit 1 on stderr, nothing merged and nothing recorded:
  lane     the open lane record ($(octo-feather-state)/lanes/*.toml) whose `pr` is PR, its name the lane's, its
           `issue` the parent; none, two or more, or one with no `issue` refuses naming the fix
  allowed  merge_allowed, the one place the merge asks whether it may go (issue-lifecycle #gates-merge-guard):
           `gh pr view PR` reads it OPEN and its head is HEAD (7+ hex prefix); a PR closed or merged, or at another
           head, refuses. Then the merge ask check, run here afresh as merge-ask runs it (never a stored print),
           with --status every docs/specs/*.status.json of the repo's top: an ask with no `## Merge ask` section, a
           Failed list it cannot read, or one merge-ask refuses outright, refuses (no tier to sort). An ask of tier
           1 or 2 waiting for nobody goes, `allowed: PR #<n> at <sha12>, its merge ask (<the tier block>)` on stderr
           (`on the director's relay ...` when a relay let it). Any other goes only on the director's relay of lane NAME's merge on
           record (relay, below) naming HEAD, or naming an earlier head when the commits between are only merges
           of main the one-head check proves clean (#lanes-one-head; printed `relay carried: ...`). No relay
           refuses, `its merge waits for <whom>` (the owner; the director's order, naming each set-back PR) with
           the tier block (its form: merge-ask) and `PR #<n> stays open`; a relay naming another head refuses
           naming the head it allows, the head given and why it does not carry
  merge    `gh pr merge PR --merge --match-head-commit <the PR's full head>`, printing `merged: PR #<n> at head
           <sha12> (lane <name>)`; a gh failure refuses, naming it
  done     `done <key>` (below), its lines, <key> the tracker key of the record's `issue`, a lane key (comms
           #lane-record), as tracker_calls.py parent_key maps it (#1249); a failure is named on stderr and the
           routine still runs
  routine  the post-merge routine for the lane, PR and HEAD (below), its lines and summary as `post-merge` prints
           them, its record written
Exit 0 when merged, done, and steps 1-7 ok (step 8 pending or ok); 1 otherwise. A merged lane's routine resumes
with `post-merge PR HEAD --lane <name>`.

  octo-feather-lane relay PR HEAD [--link <where the word is written>]
                                   the director's relay of lane PR's merge at HEAD, on record

`relay` (issue 1266; issue-lifecycle #gates-merge-guard, #gates-tier-relay) runs only in the director's pane:
HERDR_PANE_ID must be the `pane` of $(octo-feather-state)/director.toml, else it refuses, `only the director relays
a merge`, nothing recorded. The open lane record whose `pr` is PR names the lane (merge's rule above). It appends
one line to $(octo-feather-state)/director.handoffs.jsonl, {"at", "kind": "relay", "lane", "pr", "head"} and
"link" when given (stored, never required or checked), and prints `relayed: PR #<n> at head <sha12> (lane
<name>)`. merge reads these lines (above). Check class: declaration (docs/adr/0008): for a tier 3 ask the line
proves the director's relay, not the owner's word. Exit 0 recorded; 1 refused or bad arguments.

  octo-feather-lane post-merge PR HEAD --lane NAME [--wait SECONDS] [--repo O/R]
                                   the post-merge routine for lane NAME, its steps in order
  octo-feather-lane post-merge --lane NAME
                                   lane NAME's post-merge record
  octo-feather-lane post-merge --lane NAME --unit-end [--repo O/R]
                                   the teardown unit's stop hook (archive, above): its end recorded, then the
                                   director told (below)
  octo-feather-lane post-merge --map-job [--unit-run]
  octo-feather-lane post-merge --map-end
                                   step 5's map job, run in its unit, and its stop hook (step 5, below)

`post-merge` is the routine after a lane merges (owner ruling 2026-10-29 on issue 206; docs/adr/0008 Duties): `merge`
(above) runs it; `archive` (above) runs it for step 8 in its teardown unit; the director reruns it after a stop. The
director's message (issue 1134): a run that stops tells the director's pane (director.toml) the stop, `post-merge
<lane> PR #<n> stopped: step <n> <name> stopped: <line>; fix it, then rerun ...` (the run's --repo kept, and its --wait when not 300); a run whose steps are
all ok, on a record the archive recorded its part in, sends the one message for the merge: the summary, tickets done, left open,
reflection findings, correction lanes, left on disk on purpose (the teardown's kept items) and the teardown line,
` | ` between them; a teardown unit that ended before its routine finished (killed, timed out) tells that. Each
once: the record's told keeps what was told (the end, or the stop's line), and a run telling it again sends nothing
(`director already told`). Sent as step 7 sends (herdr-say --timeout 20, killed 15 s past); a failed send prints
`director NOT told: <why>; re-send by hand: herdr-say ...` (no pane in director.toml: DIRECTOR_PANE and where
to find it), exit 1, nothing recorded. Every send of the routine (step 7, the director's messages, the teardown
unit's end) is the routine's own, not its caller's: herdr-say runs without the caller's OCTO_FEATHER_ROLE and
OCTO_FEATHER_PARENT, so the up-chain guard of the supervisor whose pane runs merge or archive does not refuse it
(#1249; docs/adr/0015 holds for that supervisor's own herdr-say). One run of a lane at a
time: a second waits for the first (a lock per lane). Each run records each step it runs, its outcome, line and
time, in the lane's record, $(octo-feather-state)/post-merge/<lane>.json (its form: scripts/post_merge.py). A rerun for the same merge (its PR, and a
head one of the two is a prefix of) runs no step recorded ok before the first one not ok: each prints its recorded
line as `step <n> <name>: ok: <what> (recorded <time>, not rerun)`, and the routine resumes at the first step not
ok; a record of another merge is replaced. Each step is idempotent, and step 7 tells no lane twice. With only
--lane NAME it prints the lane's record: `post-merge record <lane>: PR #<n>, head <head>, merge commit <sha> (<file>)`,
then per step `step <n> <name>: <outcome> at <time>: <line>` or `step <n> <name>: not run`, then, when recorded,
`teardown unit <name> (<how>): started <time>, ended <time>: <result> (<status>)` (`running or ended unrecorded`
before its end) and `director told: <each>`; exit 0, 1 with no or an unreadable record.
  step 1 merged    `gh pr view PR`: the PR is MERGED, its head is HEAD (7+ hex prefix), and it has a merge commit
  step 2 main CI   `gh run list --commit <merge commit> --event push`: waits up to --wait SECONDS (default 300)
                   for a run to appear, then `gh run watch <id> --exit-status` on each run until every run gh
                   lists is completed; ok only when every run concluded success, or cancelled and superseded. A run
                   cancelled (a later push's concurrency cancel-in-progress) is superseded by the first later push
                   run of its workflow (`gh run list --branch <base> --event push --workflow <name>`, a higher run
                   id) whose headSha contains the merge commit (`gh api repos/<repo>/compare/<merge>...<headSha>`
                   ahead or identical): watched to its end, ok on success naming both runs, red stops; cancelled in
                   turn, its own successor counts; none within --wait stops as red. A red run (any other
                   conclusion) stops at once, `main CI red on <m>: <wf> <conclusion> <url>`, each rerun with that
                   same line (the director told once), until a later push run of its workflow on the base (that
                   list, read once, never waited for) containing the merge commit concluded success: the first
                   such makes it ok, `main CI red on <m>: <wf> <url>, green since in <url> on <sha12> (contains
                   <m>)`, then the other runs' line; the archive reruns it (#gates-main-moved). The no-CI
                   fact: while gh lists no run, `gh api repos/<repo>/git/trees/<merge commit>` (then .github,
                   then workflows) finds no
                   .github/workflows/*.yml or *.yaml file; then step 2 is ok at once, `no CI: no workflow file
                   ...`, with no wait. merge-ask reads the same fact at the PR head
  step 3 kit refresh  the live kit install (this script's kit dir, or env OCTO_FEATHER_KIT_INSTALL): only when
                   `gh repo view` names the PR's repo and the install's origin alike, else `kit install unchanged:
                   PR in <repo>, install is <repo>`. The live-run rule (issue-lifecycle #gates-main-moved): a
                   live run of the box's slots (heavy: gate and qc runs; eval: octo-feather-qa; live while its
                   lock is held: octo-feather-slot --help, Files) whose recorded process works in the install
                   (its cwd) or runs a program from it (its executable, argv[0] or script) is waited for, up to
                   --wait, re-read each second; then step 3 stops, nothing moved, `kit refresh waits: <kind> run
                   pid <pid> of lane <lane> since <since> reads <install>`. Where /proc is absent (macOS), or a
                   process is not read, any live run counts. Stops, nothing changed, on what issue-lifecycle
                   #gates-main-moved refuses, checked on the install and on step 4's checkout before either moves
                   (an install that is step 4's checkout as that checkout), named `kit install|checkout <path> is
                   dirty (...)`, `is on <branch|a detached HEAD>, not <base>` or `has diverged ...`; after `git
                   fetch origin`, one behind it
                   fast-forwards (`git merge --ff-only`, a detached HEAD stays detached), one at or past it does
                   not move. Then in the install, each failure stopping named: `octo-feather-mise-install .` (the
                   kit's own locked mise tools, never the user's global ones; only with a mise.toml there), `just vendored-fetch`, `uv sync --locked` (env UV_PYTHON_DOWNLOADS=never: a
                   pinned Python (.python-version) not installed stops, naming `uv python install --no-bin <pin>`),
                   `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, `just install-check <the current repo's top>`. No service
                   restart; each run of step 3 reruns these
  step 4 checkout  the director's own checkout of the target: the main checkout of the repo post-merge runs in (`git
                   worktree list`, first entry), for any target repo; when it is the kit install, step 3 is its
                   one move (`checkout <path> is the kit install, which step 3 moves`). Step 3's rules on it, nothing
                   changed when it stops, the line naming the checkout and why: dirty (git status --porcelain), on
                   a branch other than the PR's base or a detached HEAD (`is on <branch>, not <base>`), or diverged
                   from the merge commit; one behind it on the base fast-forwards (`git merge --ff-only`), one at
                   or past it does not move. `git fetch origin` only when the merge commit is missing there
  step 5 coverage map  where the repo builds a coverage map (`just --summary` in step 4's checkout names a fast-map
                   recipe; else `coverage map: skipped: <checkout> has no fast-map recipe (just --summary)`),
                   its map for the merge commit is built by the clone's one map job, never waited for (quality
                   #acceptance-map-after-merge, #acceptance-map-full-daily, #acceptance-map-build-failed). The
                   map dir, <git common dir>/octo-feather/fast-map/ (octo-feather-gate --help, map), holds its
                   record, build.json (its form: scripts/post_merge.py, step 5), under build.lock. Its line:
                   `coverage map: already done: <map file>` when the merge's map exists; `already started` or
                   `already queued` when the record has that merge; `queued: <m>, after unit <unit>'s build
                   of <r>[, replacing queued <q>, never mapped]` while a build runs (one merge queued at
                   most, a newer one replacing it); else `started: unit <unit> (<how>, limit 3600s, its lines
                   in <map dir>/build.log) builds the map for <m>; the routine goes on without it`. The job
                   is one transient unit per clone, octo-feather-fast-map-<clone id> (the teardown unit's
                   start: `systemd-run --user`, RuntimeMaxSec 3600, ExecStopPost `post-merge --map-end`;
                   where no systemd user manager answers, its own session, `post-merge --map-job
                   --unit-run`, killed past the same limit, then the same end), cwd the checkout, running
                   `post-merge --map-job`: at the lowest processor and disk priority (nice 19, ionice
                   idle), and only once a fast-run turn is free (no fast run waiting for one: the slots
                   dir's fast-I.lock, octo-feather-slot --help, Files), in a scratch worktree at the merge
                   commit (`git worktree add --detach` in the map dir, removed at the build's end) it runs
                   `just fast-map --since-newest` (re-mapping only the test items changed since the newest
                   map: the justfile's fast-map), or a full `just fast-map` when the record holds no full
                   map built less than a day before that is still an ancestor's map: a fast-tier run, no
                   heavy slot. It then builds the queued merge, if any, and ends. Each build's end goes in
                   the record (last: merge, result, why, items run, mapped, not passed, wall, CPU). A build
                   that writes no map for its merge (just failed, killed, timed out, the unit not started)
                   leaves every map as it was, so select keeps the previous one, and tells the merge's
                   lane's director once (`coverage map build for <m> (lane <lane>, PR #<n>) failed: <why>;
                   ...`, as the routine's stops are told); a unit ended while building has that recorded
                   by its stop hook, its scratch worktree removed and the queued merge dropped. Always ok:
                   it never stops the routine
  step 6 stale agents  `stale`'s report (below), its lines joined by `; `; always ok: it never stops the routine,
                   and a Herdr it cannot read is named in its line (`stale agents: not read: ...`)
  step 7 tell supervisors  each open lane record but --lane's, of the PR's repo (every lane when the PR is the kit
                   install's origin repo: every lane runs kit roles), whose supervisor pane `herdr agent get` finds
                   gets one herdr-say: "main moved to <merge sha12> (#PR <title>); rule files changed: <list or
                   none>; re-read the changed ones before your next step. (from the post-merge routine of lane
                   <lane>)", <lane> the merging lane, its sender. Rule files: what the merge commit
                   changed against its first parent (git in the current repo, fetched from origin when missing)
                   under agents/*.md, .agents/skills/, .skillfile/patches/, AGENTS.md, docs/adr/, docs/specs/.
                   A pane Herdr does not find (agent_not_found, read from stderr or stdout) is never sent to (why:
                   herdr-comms SKILL.md, herdr-say) and never stops the step: listed `not woken: <lane> <pane>`
                   (parked) when the lane's latest parked line in <lane>.handoffs.jsonl beside its record names
                   that pane, else `gone: director to resume or archive: <lane> <pane>`; any other herdr failure
                   stops the step, named. A lane whose supervisor is the merging lane's (its record, open or
                   archived: that supervisor runs this routine, mid-turn) is never sent to, listed `told: <lane>
                   <pane> (the merging supervisor)`. Each lane told gets one line in
                   $(octo-feather-state)/director.handoffs.jsonl, {"at", "kind": "prod", "issue", "note":
                   "post-merge <merge sha> #PR: main moved, told <lane> to re-read <files|none> (from the
                   post-merge routine of lane <merging lane>)"} (spec comms
                   #handoff-director-prod); a rerun skips a lane whose prod line names that merge (`already told`).
                   Each send is bounded (#619, #666): `herdr-say --timeout 20`, Herdr's own wait bound (herdr-say
                   exit 124 past it); a herdr-say still running 15 s past it is killed. A failed send (timed out,
                   exit 75 blocked, or other) is loud (director rule #619): that supervisor is NOT told, named
                   with its lane, pane, why (a timeout: `NOT told: <lane> <pane>: send timed out after 20s`) and
                   the command that re-sends it by hand (`re-send by hand: herdr-say <pane> '<message>'`); it
                   writes no prod line; the step goes on to the other supervisors, then stops. Its line:
                   `NOT told: ...; told: ...; already told: ...; not woken: ...; gone: director to resume or
                   archive: ...`
  step 8 teardown  only once $(octo-feather-state)/lanes/archive/<lane>.toml exists (`octo-feather-lane archive`
                   moves it there, then starts this step in its teardown unit), else `pending: lane <lane> not
                   archived; its archive starts this step`; an archived record whose `pr` is not PR stops. In order, each stop keeping the
                   rest: first step 3's live-run rule on a run whose recorded process works in the lane's
                   worktree (its cwd; any live run where /proc is absent), `teardown waits: <kind> run pid
                   <pid> of lane <lane> since <since> reads <worktree>`, nothing closed or removed; then
                   the lane's own space (the archived `supervisor` pane id's prefix, when no open lane
                   record's supervisor and not director.toml's `pane` has it) closed with `herdr-close <space>`,
                   else only its tab with `herdr-close <supervisor pane>`; one Herdr no longer finds is `already
                   closed`. Neither closed, nothing in another record changed, when an open lane record but
                   <lane>'s names that very pane as its `supervisor` (`kept: supervisor <pane> (tab <tab>) still
                   supervises <lane>, ...`, the tab as `herdr agent list` names it, else `(tab unknown: <why>)`)
                   or director.toml's `pane` is it (`... is the director`) (#892). The worktree
                   on branch `lane-key <record issue>` (tracker skill), from `git worktree
                   list --porcelain` in the current repo, removed with `git worktree remove` (a dirty one refused in
                   git's words), then the user's mise tracked-configs and trusted-configs links whose target is that
                   worktree or under it pruned (MISE_STATE_DIR, else mise's default; the count named); kept, named, when it is the kit install, the main worktree, or the root of a Spec
                   Chat review row (${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/spec-chat/hosting/*/registry.toml); kept, the
                   local branch with it (`kept: local branch <branch>: checked out in kept worktree <path>`), when
                   a live agent outside the space or tab closed works in it or under it (an AgentInfo `cwd` or
                   `foreground_cwd` of `herdr agent list`, read before the close): `kept: worktree <path>: <pane>
                   works in it`; that list not read, `kept: worktree <path>: who works in it unknown (<why>)`,
                   never removed blind (comms #transport-close-space). Each open PR
                   based on the branch retargeted to the merged PR's base (`gh pr edit N --base`); the local branch
                   deleted only when it is an ancestor of the merge commit; then `git push origin --delete <branch>`
                   when origin has it. Gone already prints `already`; review rows, spools and records stay
Each step prints one line as it finishes, `step <n> <name>: ok: <what>` or `step <n> <name>: stopped: <why>`;
a stopped step runs no later step. The last line is the summary, each step's outcome (ok, stopped, not run):
`post-merge <lane> PR #<n>: step 1 merged ok; step 2 main CI ok`, ending `; NOT told: <lane> <pane>, ... (step 7
names each re-send)` when step 7 failed a send. Exit 0 when every step is ok, 1 when one (not merged, merged at another head, a red run named by its URL, no run within the bound, a watch that failed,
a checkout kept, a supervisor not told, a teardown refusal), 3 when steps 1-7 are ok and step 8 is pending (the run at the merge; the
archive starts step 8), 1 with usage on stderr for bad arguments.

  octo-feather-lane stale          the live agents on a stale launch set, and those with none recorded

`stale` (issue 1079; stale and unknown: spec roles#launch-set; the record: pi-role --help, Launch set) reads
each agent `herdr agent list` names (checked against Herdr's AgentInfo, a malformed answer named), its session from
the `--session-id` of its Pi process (`herdr pane process-info`, as herdr-close --park reads it), and asks the
recorded launcher for the set now with the recorded args and working directory. Its Herdr extension (herdr-spawn's
per-launch copy, under herdr-pi/ in the state dir) is compared with what a launch would install now, a fresh
`herdr integration install pi` into a temp dir (one per report), never with the agent's own copy; that install
failing is `launches no more`. A current agent is not named.
Lines, kit paths relative to the launcher's kit, skill folders with a trailing /:
  stale: <pane> <name> role <role>: <path> changed|added|gone, ...
  stale: <pane> <name> role <role>: <role file> launches no more from <cwd> (<why>)
  unknown: <pane> <name> role <role|unknown>: no Pi launched with --session-id (started by hand)
  unknown: <pane> <name> role <role|unknown>: no launch set recorded for session <id> in <dir>
  unknown: <pane> <name> role <role|unknown>: launch-set record <file> unreadable|malformed (<what>)
  unknown: <pane> <name>: its process not read (<Herdr's error>)
  stale agents: <n> stale, <n> unknown, <n> current of <n> live
It relaunches nothing. Exit 0; 1 when Herdr's agent list cannot be read or is malformed, its one line `stale
agents: not read: <why>`. post-merge step 6 prints the same report.

  octo-feather-lane start TICKET|--new TITLE --body-file FILE --goal TITLE --outcome TEXT --brief-file FILE
                                   the director's lane start, one command, in its checkout of a target
  octo-feather-lane start ... [--shown goal|brief] [--absent goal|brief]
                                   its rerun after an unknown send, naming what the supervisor's pane shows
  octo-feather-lane start -h|--help this entry alone, nothing read or made

`start` (spec issue-lifecycle #acceptance-lane-start) first fetches the kit install's origin (the kit dir this command
runs from, or OCTO_FEATHER_KIT_INSTALL: the install post-merge's step 3 refreshes) and refuses, exit 1 with nothing
made, when main there has a commit the install lacks, naming the install, its commit, main's and the fix (post-merge's
kit refresh, or a fast-forward of the install; #acceptance-lane-start-current-kit). Then it reads the lane key (the target's tracker skill's lane-key, as
octo-feather-start resolves it), the ticket (issue-view), the repository (gh repo view) and the caller's
$HERDR_PANE_ID (the director pane), each answer validated and refused naming its field before anything is made
(arguments by argparse). The lane's parent ticket: TICKET, or with --new TITLE --body-file FILE instead (spec
issue-lifecycle #acceptance-lane-start-files) one filed once the director pane, checkout and repository are read: the
tracker skill's issue-create with that title and FILE, a top-level issue, never --parent; it prints `filed=<key>` at
once, so a start that stops later is rerun naming that key as TICKET. A ticket issue-view shows with a parent (a
sub-issue of another issue) is refused naming that parent, before anything is made (#acceptance-lane-start-sub-issue).
It refuses a lane that exists (spec #acceptance-lane-start-exists): when any of lanes/<key>.toml,
the worktree, the branch `<key>` or a Herdr workspace labelled <key> (herdr workspace list) is there and no stopped
start left it, exit 1 naming each, nothing changed, no supervisor started; fetches origin and adds the worktree `<checkout>-<key>` beside the checkout on a
new branch `<key>` from origin's main; starts the supervisor with the octo-feather-start beside this command
(`--issue TICKET --cwd WORKTREE`); writes lanes/<supervisor name>.toml (comms spec #lane-record: supervisor,
repository, issue, goal TITLE, started_at, waiting_on ""); sends the supervisor with herdr-say `/goal TEXT for <key>,
delivered through implement-spec; done when ...`, then the brief: a header (lane key, parent issue, director pane,
worktree, branch, main commit), then FILE's text; appends one director log line (comms spec
#handoff-director-prod: issue, note); prints `key=<key> pane=<pane> issue=<TICKET>` (with --new, the filed key). Each step that fails stops it,
exit 1, naming the step and what it made so far; a failed herdr-say is never retried within a run. A stopped start keeps
lanes/<key>.start.json beside the record (its ticket, title, outcome and brief's sha256, then each done step's fact:
main commit, supervisor pane, name and workspace, started_at, goal_sent, brief_sent, and goal_unknown or
brief_unknown: a send begun, or one herdr-say ended neither delivered nor rejected); the same start run again
(spec #acceptance-lane-start-rerun) continues from the step that stopped, skipping each done one, so the lane gets one
worktree, one supervisor, one record, the goal and the brief once each and one director log line; a rerun with
another title, outcome or brief is refused naming that file. A send herdr-say rejected (exit 64, 69, 75 or 77: nothing
typed) is sent on the rerun. A send left unknown (any other failure, a timeout 124 or a stall among them; comms spec
#delivery-unknown) is never sent again blind: the rerun refuses, exit 1, naming the step, the supervisor's pane and
what to look at there; look, then rerun the same start with --shown STEP when the pane shows the message (counted
sent), or --absent STEP when it does not and the pane is not compacting (sent once more). Naming either for a step
with no unknown send is refused. Once the start finishes the file is gone, and a rerun
is refused as any existing lane.

One subcommand per function, listed in COMMANDS; add a subcommand there.
